'''alma mater''' (al′mä mā′tėr). [L.: ''alma'', fem. of ''almus'', fostering, cherishing, benign, < ''alere'', nourish, foster (see ''aliment'' and ''alumnus''); ''mater'' = E. ''mother''.] Literally, fostering mother: in modern use, applied by students to the university or college in which they have been trained.

{{smaller block|Benjamin Woodbridge was the eldest son of our ''alma mater''.
{{right|''Peirce'', Hist. of Harv. Univ., App., p. 57.}}}}

'''Alman†''' (al′man), ''a.'' and ''n.'' Same as ''Almain.''

